00:12:00,220 --> 00:12:07,220
 So hopefully, that paints a picture of what the Lambda universe looks like today and sort of allays some of the confusion.

122
00:12:07,220 --> 00:12:15,220
 But it really is a reimagining of Lambda to be able to take advantage of more workloads, which ultimately will help you in the applications that you build.

123
00:12:15,220 --> 00:12:21,220
 So let's cover event functions and actually more durable functions and what that functionality is.

124
00:12:21,220 --> 00:12:30,220
 So in, again, the good old times, monoliths would sometimes get a bad rap, but were actually pretty easy to reason about.

125
00:12:30,220 --> 00:12:32,220
 You had a big chunk of code.

126
00:12:32,220 --> 00:12:33,220
 It ran on a server.

127
00:12:33,220 --> 00:12:34,220
 Maybe it ran in the cloud.

128
00:12:34,220 --> 00:12:35,220
 Maybe it ran on premises.

129
00:12:35,220 --> 00:12:38,220
 It doesn't matter, but you had a big application.

130
00:12:38,220 --> 00:12:39,220
 It was all in one place.

131
00:12:39,220 --> 00:12:43,220
 If you wanted to see the code, you could see it in, you know, one repo, one file.

132
00:12:43,220 --> 00:12:45,220
 You could do step three debugging.

133
00:12:45,220 --> 00:12:46,220
 And that was great.

134
00:12:46,220 --> 00:12:51,220
 The problem was, if you needed to scale it or you needed to use some other kind of services,

135
00:12:51,220 --> 00:12:53,220
 now things started to get hard.

136
00:12:53,220 --> 00:12:58,220
 You either need to have a bigger box or you need to have more boxes and then stick load balances in front of it.

137
00:12:58,220 --> 00:13:00,220
 And now you're looking after load balances.

138
00:13:00,220 --> 00:13:01,220
 And now you're looking after sticky sessions.

139
00:13:01,220 --> 00:13:04,220
 And now you're looking after a whole bunch of stuff that you don't want to do.

140
00:13:04,220 --> 00:13:09,220
 And so the world came out, and this wasn't an AWS thing, of the concept of microservices.

141
00:13:09,220 --> 00:13:14,220
 So why don't we break up that monolith in a few different parts and we could use different

142
00:13:14,220 --> 00:13:17,220
 services maybe for different jobs or different programming languages.

143
00:13:17,220 --> 00:13:21,220
 We would then need to think about communication channels between those.

144
00:13:21,220 --> 00:13:24,220
 how does one microservice talk to another microservice.

145
00:13:24,220 --> 00:13:25,220
 And that was great for the scalability.

146
00:13:25,220 --> 00:13:31,220
 But obviously more lines and boxes mean more things to manage, more things to look after.

147
00:13:31,220 --> 00:13:35,220
 And life, I think, everyone with microservices did get a little bit more complicated.

148
00:13:35,220 --> 00:13:42,220
 Some of the service purists, I think, took microservices to the end game, which was maybe a game

149
00:13:42,220 --> 00:13:46,220
 of how many Lambda functions you could possibly create for an application.

150
00:13:46,220 --> 00:13:48,220
 And that became just a nightmare to manage.

151
00:13:48,220 --> 00:13:53,220
 So it's obviously a middle ground between having 2B microservices and middle ground and somewhere

152
00:13:53,220 --> 00:13:55,220
 in between which works for you.

153
00:13:55,220 --> 00:14:02,220
 But what if we could have a single function and we could see it as a monolithic code base,

154
00:14:02,220 --> 00:14:08,220
 but it could take advantage of a distributed system and run like a distributed application.

155
00:14:08,220 --> 00:14:13,220
 And this is one of the core fundamentals of AWS Lambda Durable Functions.

156
00:14:13,220 --> 00:14:17,220
 Where you have a workflow, and a workflow is a process that you're going to run.

157
00:14:17,220 --> 00:14:19,220
 So maybe there's going to be a transaction.

158
00:14:19,220 --> 00:14:21,220
 Maybe there's going to be multiple steps.

159
00:14:21,220 --> 00:14:23,220
 Maybe there's going to map over something.

160
00:14:23,220 --> 00:14:24,220
 Maybe it's if-then statements.

161
00:14:24,220 --> 00:14:26,220
 All these kind of things that you run.

162
00:14:26,220 --> 00:14:29,220
 Workflows, which people have been doing for ages in code.

163
00:14:29,220 --> 00:14:33,220
 And now this makes it a lot easier with durable functions.

164
00:14:33,220 --> 00:14:38,220
 So Guber is actually going to do a way better demo of durable functions a little bit later.

165
00:14:38,220 --> 00:14:41,220
 So I don't want to steal his thunder, but I'm just quickly going to go through.

166
00:14:41,220 --> 00:14:46,220
 So hopefully you can understand the fundamentals of that before Guber shows you the real good stuff.

167
00:14:46,220 --> 00:14:51,220
 So the concept, it's called a durable function because it's using what's called a durable execution.

168
00:14:51,220 --> 00:14:58,220
 And a durable execution is quite amazing if you think about it, but quite simple, which is a good thing.

169
00:14:58,220 --> 00:15:03,220
 And basically, as your code runs, every now and again you just go save state.

170
00:15:03,220 --> 00:15:09,220
 If something then dies, or your lambda function dies, or downstream service dies, or whatever,

171
00:15:09,220 --> 00:15:15,220
 when the function restarts, all it can do is just catch up where it was initially saved.

172
00:15:15,220 --> 00:15:19,220
 And it's a very simple concept, but think how useful that can be.

173
00:15:19,220 --> 00:15:22,220
 And the analogy I like to use is think of a save game.

174
00:15:22,220 --> 00:15:25,220
 So if there are any gamers in the house, there must be gamers in the house.

175
00:15:25,220 --> 00:15:29,220
 So it's a bit early in the morning, but some nervous people are putting their hands up.

176
00:15:29,220 --> 00:15:33,220
 Can you imagine if you're playing a game, and you hit save, and you finish your level, and the game's saved.

177
00:15:33,220 --> 00:15:42,220
 The next time you come up to play the game, it starts from the last game stage that was automatically saved, and you carry on.

178
00:15:42,220 --> 00:15:45,220
 That's far more pleasurable than every time you start a game.

179
00:15:45,220 --> 00:15:47,220
 You have to start at the beginning, and build all your way up.

180
00:15:47,220 --> 00:15:49,220
 Same kind of thing with durable functions.

181
00:15:49,220 --> 00:15:55,220
 So it allows you, with your control, to just create a checkpoint in different parts of your software to say,

182
00:15:55,220 --> 00:15:59,220
 I've connected to that API, got the response back, save that response.

183
00:15:59,220 --> 00:16:01,220
 Connect to that database, save that response.

184
00:16:01,220 --> 00:16:07,220
 I've done a call to an LLM model, LLM, come back, which is non-deterministic, let me save that response.

185
00:16:07,220 --> 00:16:11,220
 Something happens, and it just replays through all the different processes, and does that.

186
00:16:11,220 --> 00:16:14,220
 And the way that it does that, is there's just an SDK.

187
00:16:14,220 --> 00:16:18,220
 On land, you basically tick a box to say, yes, I want this to be a durable function.

188
00:16:18,220 --> 00:16:21,220
 You install the SDK, you get the superpowers.

189
00:16:21,220 --> 00:16:25,220
 It's available for Node.js, Python, Java, and C#.

190
00:16:25,220 --> 00:16:32,220
 And we're working in the open source as well to build some for Go and Rust, I think, are the next two that are being looked at.

191
00:16:32,220 --> 00:16:35,220
 So let's just go through a quick, durable function.

192
00:16:35,220 --> 00:16:38,220
 So in this example, we are going to be handling receipts.

193
00:16:38,220 --> 00:16:40,220
 So we are doing expense claims.

194
00:16:40,220 --> 00:16:43,220
 Some of you people may be doing that as well here today.

195
00:16:43,220 --> 00:16:44,220
 So this is what it would look like.

196
00:16:44,220 --> 00:16:46,220
 On the left-hand side, we've got some pseudocode.

197
00:16:46,220 --> 00:16:50,220
 You can see there's some different things in that, like CTX, which is for context.

198
00:16:50,220 --> 00:17:00,220
 CTX.map is the way that you're able to communicate with Lambda to be able to use that context to be able to step through and run your application.

199
00:17:00,220 --> 00:17:04,220
 And on the right-hand side is a log of what's happening within the durable functions.

200
00:17:04,220 --> 00:17:08,220
 And you can see we've got four photos that we've uploaded into the system.

201
00:17:08,220 --> 00:17:11,220
 Photos one and two that we processed correctly.

202
00:17:11,220 --> 00:17:14,220
 Photo four, the little OCR thing, is a little bit blurry.

203
00:17:14,220 --> 00:17:18,220
 But within your durable function, you've configured to say, don't worry about it.

204
00:17:18,220 --> 00:17:20,220
 You don't necessarily have to have all of the work in.

205
00:17:20,220 --> 00:17:22,220
 You know, three out of four is absolutely fine.

206
00:17:22,220 --> 00:17:25,220
 We don't want this expense claim to fail just for one blurry photo.

207
00:17:25,220 --> 00:17:28,220
 We want to, you know, let the other three go to the system.

208
00:17:28,220 --> 00:17:35,220
 Tell the user at the end, and they can just, you know, once they upload another photo, they can then use that as another expense.

209
00:17:35,220 --> 00:17:36,220
 Or just an example.

210
00:17:36,220 --> 00:17:38,220
 But there's an issue with photo number three.

211
00:17:38,220 --> 00:17:40,220
 We don't know what that is.

212
00:17:40,220 --> 00:17:41,220
 Something dies.

213
00:17:41,220 --> 00:17:43,220
 Maybe it's a failed execution environment.

214
00:17:43,220 --> 00:17:47,220
 Maybe it's the OCR connection at the back, or whatever NLM is being called.

215
00:17:47,220 --> 00:17:49,220
 And that call has failed.

216
00:17:49,220 --> 00:17:54,220
 So what's going to happen is, when it fails, the Lambda function is going to recover.

217
00:17:54,220 --> 00:17:57,220
 You can do that automatically, or you can choose that kind of things.

218
00:17:57,220 --> 00:18:00,220
 And you can see, it's not going to do anything for photos one, two, and four.

219
00:18:00,220 --> 00:18:01,220
 Because it's handled that.

220
00:18:01,220 --> 00:18:03,220
 It doesn't need to do an NLM call.

221
00:18:03,220 --> 00:18:04,220
 It doesn't need to do OCR anymore.

222
00:18:04,220 --> 00:18:06,220
 And it's just going to reprocess photo number three.

223
00:18:06,220 --> 00:18:12,220
 So, really easy to just be able to catch up and fix errors that are happening automatically.

224
00:18:12,220 --> 00:18:14,220
 You can also run things in parallel.

225
00:18:14,220 --> 00:18:16,220
 So you can see ctx.parallels.

226
00:18:16,220 --> 00:18:19,220
 That's a way that you can say, I need to check the policy of this.

227
00:18:19,220 --> 00:18:24,220
 Some kind of call to say, you know, is this receipt allowed?

228
00:18:24,220 --> 00:18:26,220
 We're going to check the duplicates.

229
00:18:26,220 --> 00:18:28,220
 Was this expense claim done last week as well?

230
00:18:28,220 --> 00:18:30,220
 So you can run multiple checks in parallel.

231
00:18:30,220 --> 00:18:33,220
 You can think of this if you're doing code reviews, or you're doing any kind of processing.

232
00:18:33,220 --> 00:18:37,220
 There are often lots of things you can do at the same time for efficiency.

233
00:18:37,220 --> 00:18:39,220
 Then the next thing is a callback.

234
00:18:39,220 --> 00:18:41,220
 So you can see ctx.callback.

235
00:18:41,220 --> 00:18:47,220
 And this is, well, this is say over 500 pounds, 1,000 pounds, whatever your example is with this.

236
00:18:47,220 --> 00:18:49,220
 And we actually need to get a manager approval.

237
00:18:49,220 --> 00:18:53,220
 And so what's going to happen is, the Lambda function, the Google function is just going to stop.

238
00:18:53,220 --> 00:18:57,220
 It's not going to do anything, and it can wait for actually up to a year.

239
00:18:57,220 --> 00:18:58,220
 So there we go.

240
00:18:58,220 --> 00:19:02,220
 Instead of the 15 minutes for an individual Lambda function invoke, we've got now a weighted

241
00:19:02,220 --> 00:19:04,220
 Lambda function that can run up to 15 years.

242
00:19:04,220 --> 00:19:10,220
 In fact, each of these different blocks, which is running a Lambda process, can run up to 15 minutes itself.

243
00:19:10,220 --> 00:19:15,220
 But the whole thing, you know, can pause and run for up to a year with all these individual steps.

244
00:19:15,220 --> 00:19:18,220
 So we don't know how the manager is going to approve it.

245
00:19:18,220 --> 00:19:19,220
 Maybe they go to a website.

246
00:19:19,220 --> 00:19:21,220
 Maybe they reply to an email or a Slack trade or something.

247
00:19:21,220 --> 00:19:25,220
 But anyway, some kind of thing talks back to Lambda and says, yeah, I've approved that.

248
00:19:25,220 --> 00:19:28,220
 And then the Lambda function just automatically continues.

249
00:19:28,220 --> 00:19:30,220
 Obviously, you're not paying for that waiting time.

250
00:19:30,220 --> 00:19:33,220
 The function is just going to pause and suspend.

251
00:19:33,220 --> 00:19:34,220
 And be free.

252
00:19:34,220 --> 00:19:36,220
 Lambda, the manager approves it.

253
00:19:36,220 --> 00:19:38,220
 And off you go and it carries on.

254
00:19:38,220 --> 00:19:40,220
 The last step is obviously you want to pay it out.

255
00:19:40,220 --> 00:19:42,220
 But here there's a problem with the payment provider.

256
00:19:42,220 --> 00:19:44,220
 A failed payment.

257
00:19:44,220 --> 00:19:45,220
 What are we going to do?

258
00:19:45,220 --> 00:19:49,220
 So, as you may have worked out before, the whole rest of the Lambda function can rerun again.

259
00:19:49,220 --> 00:19:51,220
 You don't need to check the photos.

260
00:19:51,220 --> 00:19:52,220
 You don't need to check the policy.

261
00:19:52,220 --> 00:19:54,220
 You don't need to do the manager approval.

262
00:19:54,220 --> 00:19:55,220
 That has all been done before.

263
00:19:55,220 --> 00:20:00,220
 Each of those steps are basically stored in an external database of Lambda managers.

264
00:20:00,220 --> 00:20:03,220
 And when the log is replayed, it goes done, done, done.

265
00:20:03,220 --> 00:20:04,220
 Tick, tick, tick, tick, tick, tick, tick.

266
00:20:04,220 --> 00:20:05,220
 All done.

267
00:20:05,220 --> 00:20:07,220
 I just need to do the payout.

268
00:20:07,220 --> 00:20:11,220
 And this is great because you don't need to then run all of these if-then statements,

269
00:20:11,220 --> 00:20:16,220
 all of this complex code to be able to undo the transaction, to be able to redo the workflow

270
00:20:16,220 --> 00:20:20,220
 and all different kind of things and say, you know, if photo one has been done, do this.

271
00:20:20,220 --> 00:20:24,220
 This is all just handled for you as part of the service.

272
00:20:24,220 --> 00:20:26,220
 Item potency is built in as well.

273
00:20:26,220 --> 00:20:27,220
 Something like a payment claim.

274
00:20:27,220 --> 00:20:31,220
 When you're doing an expense claim, I suppose I wouldn't mind if I was paid twice.

275
00:20:31,220 --> 00:20:34,220
 But if it was your credit card being charged, you don't want to pay twice as well.

276
00:20:34,220 --> 00:20:40,220
 So using an item potency key, which is just built into the service, allows things to only happen at once.

277
00:20:40,220 --> 00:20:45,220
 Again, goodness going to carry through and do a whole bunch of different kind of information on this as well.

278
00:20:45,220 --> 00:20:50,220
 So then the clever ones of you are thinking, well, we do already have a service called Step Functions.

279
00:20:50,220 --> 00:20:52,220
 What's that all about?

280
00:20:52,220 --> 00:20:54,220
 Well, Step Functions is still around.

281
00:20:54,220 --> 00:20:55,220
 It is loved.

282
00:20:55,220 --> 00:20:56,220
 It is huge.

283
00:20:56,220 --> 00:20:59,220
 Used hugely internally at Amazon as well.

284
00:20:59,220 --> 00:21:02,220
 And they're two different approaches for running workflows.

285
00:21:02,220 --> 00:21:05,220
 Step Functions isn't on the way out.

286
00:21:05,220 --> 00:21:06,220
 Lots of investments going in that.

287
00:21:06,220 --> 00:21:08,220
 A big team which runs that as well.

288
00:21:08,220 --> 00:21:13,220
 But what Step Functions is really good at is basically orchestrating across AWS services.

289
00:21:13,220 --> 00:21:16,220
 Because it has the AWS SDK just built in.

290
00:21:16,220 --> 00:21:25,220
 So any AWS SDK call, be that writing to DynamoDB, starting a glue job, connecting to some synchronous process.

291
00:21:25,220 --> 00:21:30,220
 Literally absolutely anything the SDK can do is built into Step Functions.

292
00:21:30,220 --> 00:21:34,220
 Which means you don't actually need to write any code to do that.

293
00:21:34,220 --> 00:21:42,220
 And so Step Functions, I think the better use case is codeless, although it's a horrible term because there's a configuration code in there.

294
00:21:42,220 --> 00:21:46,220
 But this is about orchestrating across a lot of different AWS services.

295
00:21:46,220 --> 00:21:48,220
 Step Functions has got a visual builder.

296
00:21:48,220 --> 00:21:51,220
 And of course there's no catching all the things to update.

297
00:21:51,220 --> 00:22:00,220
 However, if your Step Functions state machine looked like a whole bunch of coordinating just Lambda functions, that is fine.

298
00:22:00,220 --> 00:22:04,220
 But sometimes, some people feel that it's actually easier just to run in a Lambda function.

299
00:22:04,220 --> 00:22:06,220
 So you can do Step 3 debugging.

300
00:22:06,220 --> 00:22:10,220
 You can run it in the Durable Functions emulator, which runs locally.

301
00:22:10,220 --> 00:22:16,220
 And it just makes a little bit more sense that you can see all the code within your individual Lambda function.

302
00:22:16,220 --> 00:22:24,220
 And that branching logic, and that calling an LLM, or that doing any photo processing, makes a little bit more sense inside the actual Lambda function.

303
00:22:25,220 --> 00:22:29,220
 Obviously that means you're writing code in your own programming language.

304
00:22:29,220 --> 00:22:34,220
 Some people prefer that, rather than using the Amazon state's language with Step Functions that it does.

305
00:22:34,220 --> 00:22:36,220
 Again, just part of your function.

306
00:22:36,220 --> 00:22:38,220
 So different ways of doing it.

307
00:22:38,220 --> 00:22:44,220
 But yes, Step Functions still usually use a lot of IT automation steps where they aren't code kind of things.

308
00:22:44,220 --> 00:22:45,220
 Or user onboarding.

309
00:22:45,220 --> 00:22:48,220
 Where they're creating cognitive user pools.

310
00:22:48,220 --> 00:22:52,220
 Or spinning up services for a CI/CD pipeline or a test.

311
00:22:52,220 --> 00:22:55,220
 If it is coordinating those services, Step Functions is still great.

312
00:22:55,220 --> 00:22:57,220
 But we now have another option.

313
00:22:57,220 --> 00:23:01,220
 Rather than you having to learn Step Functions language, you can do everything in code.

314
00:23:01,220 --> 00:23:03,220
 So that's Durable Functions.

315
00:23:03,220 --> 00:23:07,220
 So a week and a day ago, we then came out with Web Functions.

316
00:23:07,220 --> 00:23:11,220
 Which is just a standard HTTP server.

317
00:23:11,220 --> 00:23:17,220
 So the question that started all of this is, what if Lambda just listened on a port?

318
00:23:17,220 --> 00:23:22,220
 And you can see the left hand side, that is what a Lambda event handler looks like.

319
00:23:22,220 --> 00:23:27,220
 You can see an event comes in and you return a status body and a body that comes up in JSON.

320
00:23:27,220 --> 00:23:30,220
 But Web frameworks don't look like that.

321
00:23:30,220 --> 00:23:35,220
 If any of you are web developers, you actually start a web server that listens on a port.

322
00:23:35,220 --> 00:23:40,220
 And then requests are going to come in to that port, maybe via load balancer, maybe directly.

323
00:23:40,220 --> 00:23:44,220
 It's just a server process that is running via HTTP.

324
00:23:44,220 --> 00:23:46,220
 And there are a lot of benefits of that.

325
00:23:46,220 --> 00:23:53,220
 And if you were previously running web services on Lambda, you either had to not run a web server and adopt the event functions model.

326
00:23:53,220 --> 00:23:57,220
 Or you had to run your web server but then use the Lambda web adapter as a translation layer.

327
00:23:57,220 --> 00:24:03,220
 HTTP would come in, it would turn it into an event model and the adapter would turn it back into HTTP.

328
00:24:03,220 --> 00:24:08,220
 Again, clunky stuff that you need to do for Lambda. Unnecessary.

329
00:24:08,220 --> 00:24:13,220
 So what Lambda web functions give you is standard HTTP.

330
00:24:13,220 --> 00:24:15,220
 You actually run an HTTP server.

331
00:24:15,220 --> 00:24:20,220
 Again, that sounds like heresy, but we're bringing a bit of the servers back into servers.

332
00:24:20,220 --> 00:24:25,220
 But you are running not the servers that you're going to manage, but the server process that is running.

333
00:24:25,220 --> 00:24:28,220
 And so you no longer need any adapter layer.

334
00:24:28,220 --> 00:24:34,220
 So popular frameworks, whether you're using Express, Fastify, Hono, React Router, anything like that.

335
00:24:34,220 --> 00:24:37,220
 As it runs locally, it can run locally.

336
00:24:37,220 --> 00:24:38,220
 NPM will start.

337
00:24:38,220 --> 00:24:39,220
 I'm going to show you shortly.

338
00:24:39,220 --> 00:24:43,220
 You just upload all that code to Lambda and it's just going to run in Lambda.

339
00:24:43,220 --> 00:24:44,220
 It's got streaming built in.

340
00:24:44,220 --> 00:24:47,220
 Not only response streaming, but also request streaming.

341
00:24:47,220 --> 00:24:53,220
 So you can imagine you've got a client streaming data to your Lambda function because it is a more continuous running process.

342
00:24:53,220 --> 00:24:55,220
 And then sending that back.

343
00:24:55,220 --> 00:24:59,220
 So it's no longer the Lambda event model where something comes in and Lambda freezes the environment.

344
00:24:59,220 --> 00:25:06,220
 We actually keep that server running around for a few minutes at a time to be able to do that as well.

345
00:25:06,220 --> 00:25:09,220
 And a big winner as well for two reasons is it just runs low key.

346
00:25:09,220 --> 00:25:17,220
 So you do your NPM start, NPM run dev, and check your web function runs low key and then push it up to Lambda and then no code changes require.

347
00:25:17,220 --> 00:25:25,220
 And that's super useful because in the days of ALMs writing and testing a whole bunch of things, ALMs are super great at writing web servers.

348
00:25:25,220 --> 00:25:30,220
 They know that, you know, all of the models have been trained on web servers, there's new information coming as well.

349
00:25:30,220 --> 00:25:35,220
 And so you'll be able to test and iterate locally rather than having to run a Lambda emulator.

350
00:25:35,220 --> 00:25:41,220
 It's just much faster and much more sort of native and intuitive that you don't have to do this weird thing for Lambda.

351
00:25:42,220 --> 00:25:49,220
 Some other things that are part of it is Lambda Web Functions now actually allows many requests per environment.

352
00:25:49,220 --> 00:25:55,220
 So previously I spoke about the Lambda event model where you have just a single event coming into a Lambda function.

353
00:25:55,220 --> 00:26:02,220
 Well now you can have many. So you can by default 64 up to 128, you are running the server process, many requests can come in.

354
00:26:02,220 --> 00:26:08,220
 Why is that important? Well, because first of all it is native to how web servers run.

355
00:26:08,220 --> 00:26:11,220
 Web servers are used to having many requests come in, so that's just the normal way.

356
00:26:11,220 --> 00:26:22,220
 And then also for reducing cold starts. Yes, you're going to have a cold start for the very first request, but then that cold start is now amortized over 64 or 128 requests that comes in.

357
00:26:22,220 --> 00:26:24,220
 Really much more efficient.

358
00:26:24,220 --> 00:26:30,220
 In terms of configuration, a Lambda micro VM just comes with two virtual CPUs and two giga RAM.

359
00:26:30,220 --> 00:26:35,220
 And that's just what's built in one of the execution environments.

360
00:26:35,220 --> 00:26:41,220
 If that's only one execution environment, obviously Lambda can scale up to many, many execution environments to run it as well.

361
00:26:41,220 --> 00:26:47,220
 The next was the billing. So normal event Lambda, you are billed exactly for the time.

362
00:26:47,220 --> 00:26:52,220
 Web functions is slightly different and hopefully cheaper for some workloads as well.

363
00:26:52,220 --> 00:26:59,220
 So for web functions, you do pay a base level of memory, but it's not the memory that you configure, because that's a fixed amount.

364
00:26:59,220 --> 00:27:01,220
 It's just the memory that is actually actively used.

365
00:27:01,220 --> 00:27:08,220
 So we look at the memory over a minute in time and then figure out which memory you've actually used and charge you for that.

366
00:27:08,220 --> 00:27:15,220
 There's a request charge as well, same with event Lambda, but then for CPU, we only charge you the CPU when something is actually run.

367
00:27:15,220 --> 00:27:22,220
 So if you're doing a database call and then coming back and doing some calculations on that or doing a hash function or Jensen interpretation

368
00:27:22,220 --> 00:27:30,220
 or reading something from a database and transforming it into something in HTML, any of that, when the CPU is doing something, you're going to be charged for that.

369
00:27:30,220 --> 00:27:33,220
 But as soon as the CPU drops, well, you're not being charged anymore.

370
00:27:33,220 --> 00:27:37,220
 So you are kicking off a background job or doing some longer query.

371
00:27:37,220 --> 00:27:40,220
 Of course, now there's doing an LLM call where you do the call.

372
00:27:40,220 --> 00:27:45,220
 You know, Bedrock or Anthropic or OpenAI is doing all that number crunching.

373
00:27:45,220 --> 00:27:50,220
 Your web function is just going to sit there and go, "Cool, cool. I'm not actually being charged with the CPU."

374
00:27:50,220 --> 00:27:52,220
 So that is super useful.

375
00:27:52,220 --> 00:27:55,220
 The next thing is being able to build more global applications.

376
00:27:55,220 --> 00:27:59,220
 Specifically in the web world, you have customers all over the world.

377
00:27:59,220 --> 00:28:04,220
 So you can selectively choose which regions you want to run your functions in.

378
00:28:04,220 --> 00:28:09,220
 And Lambda is going to take the code and the configuration and replicate that code to multiple regions.

379
00:28:09,220 --> 00:28:14,220
 It is a particular region, so it's not the concept of, oh, you're just going to run it everywhere in the world.

380
00:28:14,220 --> 00:28:23,220
 No, you're going to pick the exact regions that you want it to run in based on your residency requirements or your data sovereignty needs.

381
00:28:23,220 --> 00:28:26,220
 And then there are actually sort of two ways that you can reach to that global environment.

382
00:28:26,220 --> 00:28:32,220
 One is a multi-region endpoint where we just send the request to the one with the shortest latency.

383
00:28:32,220 --> 00:28:35,220
 And you've basically got a single URL that you connect to.

384
00:28:35,220 --> 00:28:36,220
 I'm going to show that shortly.

385
00:28:36,220 --> 00:28:39,220
 The other one is a regional endpoint where you do have each different function.

386
00:28:39,220 --> 00:28:42,220
 It's got its own URL and you decide how you want to route that.

387
00:28:42,220 --> 00:28:47,220
 So maybe you're going to have Route 53 or you're going to have some other load balancer or some other router in it.

388
00:28:47,220 --> 00:28:52,220
 But behind the scenes, Lambda does that replication and then you have all how stuff gets to your code.

389
00:28:52,220 --> 00:28:54,220
 So some flexibility.

390
00:28:54,220 --> 00:28:59,220
 And the other cool thing is we've also added built-in canary deploys or blue-green deployments.

391
00:28:59,220 --> 00:29:01,220
 So you can have a one Lambda function version one.

392
00:29:01,220 --> 00:29:03,220
 You then go to version two.

393
00:29:03,220 --> 00:29:08,220
 And you can say I want 90% to go to version one and only 10% to go to version two.

394
00:29:08,220 --> 00:29:09,220
 See how that works.

395
00:29:09,220 --> 00:29:10,220
 Check your metrics.

396
00:29:10,220 --> 00:29:12,220
 Checks everything that's working.

397
00:29:12,220 --> 00:29:15,220
 And then just for the configuration change, you can switch to 50-50.

398
00:29:15,220 --> 00:29:16,220
 You switch it across.

399
00:29:16,220 --> 00:29:18,220
 That's just now built to the service.

400
00:29:18,220 --> 00:29:20,220
 So you don't have to use something else.

401
00:29:20,220 --> 00:29:21,220
 So yeah.

402
00:29:21,220 --> 00:29:25,220
 That's to safely be able to evolve and deploy your new version of your Lambda function.

403
00:29:25,220 --> 00:29:28,220
 So why don't we see this at work.

404
00:29:28,220 --> 00:29:34,220
 So I have a dashboard and it's called SSE because it's got server-side events.

405
00:29:34,220 --> 00:29:39,220
 So previously you could not do this with Lambda because it didn't support server-side events.

406
00:29:39,220 --> 00:29:40,220
 So we've got the code up on the screen.

407
00:29:40,220 --> 00:29:43,220
 Is that readable on the bigger screens or do I need it bigger?

408
00:29:43,220 --> 00:29:44,220
 Yes?

409
00:29:44,220 --> 00:29:45,220
 That's okay.

410
00:29:45,220 --> 00:29:46,220
 Cool.

411
00:29:46,220 --> 00:29:48,220
 Yell bigger if it needs to be bigger.

412
00:29:48,220 --> 00:29:51,220
 So I have a web function over here.

413
00:29:51,220 --> 00:29:54,220
 And as you can see, it is listening on a port.

